        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Can You Still Buy a Weslo Treadmill? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Not easily, and not on Amazon.</strong> On 9 November 2026 a search of Amazon US
            for Weslo treadmills returned replacement belts, safety keys and motors for Weslo
            machines, but no new Weslo treadmill. Walmart was always Weslo’s main retailer, and we
            could not confirm a new Weslo treadmill listed there either: independent reviews describe
            the Cadence R 5.2 as discontinued and the Cadence G 5.9i as available mainly second-hand.
            We found no announcement of new Weslo treadmills, and our own review treats the line
            as retired; a listing may reappear, but we would not plan around it.
          </p>
          <p>
            For a buyer, that leaves three routes. If you own a Weslo that still runs, a new belt,
            key or lubricant can keep it going cheaply, and the parts are on Amazon. If you want a
            used one, our <a href="/weslo-cadence-g-5-9-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Weslo Cadence G 5.9 review</a> sets out what one is worth and what to check. And if you want a new machine in the same
            spirit, the closest thing on Amazon is a <strong>NordicTrack T Series 5</strong>, built by
            the same parent company, at about $470, or a folding treadmill with powered incline from
            Goplus or THERUN for less.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-made-weslo" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Made Weslo, and Where It Was Sold</h2>
<p>
            Weslo was one of the brands of ICON Health & Fitness, the Utah company that renamed
            itself iFIT and that also owns NordicTrack, ProForm and, historically, the licensed
            Gold’s Gym, Reebok and HealthRider home-equipment lines. Within that family Weslo sat at
            the bottom of the price ladder. Its treadmills used the same basic engineering as cheap
            ProForm models, simplified: small DC motors around 2 to 2.25 HP, belts of about 16 by 50
            inches, two or three positions of manual incline, a simple LCD console and a folding
            frame.
          </p>
          <p>
            Weslo machines were sold mainly through mass retailers, Walmart above all, rather than
            through specialist fitness shops or Weslo’s own website. That is why so many owners
            describe their Weslo as “the Walmart treadmill”, and why there was never much of a Weslo
            presence on Amazon. Our guide to <a href="/walmart-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Walmart treadmills</a> covers what Walmart sells now.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The models you will see second-hand</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Cadence G 5.9 and G 5.9i.</strong> The best-known Weslo, a folding treadmill
            with a 2.25 HP motor, a 16 by 50-inch belt and two manual incline positions. The G 5.9i
            is a separate variant with its own model numbers, not the same machine with a new
            name.</li>
            <li><strong>Cadence R 5.2.</strong> A lighter entry model, described as discontinued by
            independent reviewers.</li>
            <li><strong>Cadence G-40, LX45, LS10 and LX15.</strong> Older Cadence models that still
            appear in replacement-belt listings, which tells you they are still in use.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Weslo’s model numbers start with WLTL, and that number, printed on a sticker on the
            frame, matters more than the model name when you buy parts. Belt listings on Amazon quote
            the WLTL numbers they fit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Weslo Parts and Amazon Alternatives in the Same Tier</h2>
<p>
            Because Amazon sells no new Weslo treadmill, the cards below are of two kinds. The first,
            a replacement belt for the Cadence G 5.9, is for owners. The other five are treadmills on
            Amazon in the price band Weslo used to occupy, each labelled as an alternative.
          </p>
          <p>
            We ranked the alternatives by how closely they match what a Weslo buyer wanted: a
            folding, motorised treadmill with some incline, a belt long enough for brisk walking, a
            known maker, and a price well under $1,000. Two come from iFIT, Weslo’s own parent
            company; the others are budget brands with powered incline Weslo never offered.
          </p>
        </div>
