          <p>
            The striking change since Weslo’s heyday is incline. A Weslo gave you two positions set by
            hand. Every powered alternative here raises the deck with a motor, from buttons, and most
            reach 10% or more. At the same time, prices in this tier have risen: a Weslo was often
            sold for under $400, and the iFIT machines that replace it now cost $470 to $600.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="owners" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>If You Own a Weslo: Keeping It Running</h2>
<p>
            A working Weslo is worth looking after. The motor and frame of a Cadence are simple, and
            most of the faults owners report come from wear parts that are cheap to replace.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Belt slipping, hesitating or squealing.</strong> Often a dry or worn belt.
            Lubricate first, following the manual; if the belt is frayed, glazed or thin, replace it.
            A new belt for the G 5.9 was under $100 on Amazon on 9 November. Our guide to <a href="/best-treadmill-replacement-belts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill replacement belts</a> explains how to match one by model number and measure the loop.</li>
            <li><strong>Treadmill will not start.</strong> Check the safety key first. Weslo keys are
            specific parts; the G 5.9 uses part number 208603, and a TreadLife listing for that key
            was $13.95 on Amazon.</li>
            <li><strong>Belt drifting to one side.</strong> Adjust the rear roller bolts in small
            quarter turns with the belt running slowly, as the manual describes.</li>
            <li><strong>Burning smell, tripping breaker or surging speed.</strong> Stop using it. These
            point to the motor, the motor control board or the wiring, which are the expensive
            repairs on any treadmill and, on a discontinued model, the ones where parts may simply
            run out.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The rule of thumb is to spend on wear parts and stop at the electronics. A belt, a key and
            a bottle of silicone lubricant cost far less than a new treadmill. A motor controller for
            an old ICON machine, if you can find one, plus the time or labour to fit it, can approach
            the price of a new T Series 5. Our <a href="/treadmill-repair-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill repair guide</a> helps with that decision.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-used" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Should You Buy a Used Weslo?</h2>
<p>
            At the right price, a used Weslo can still make sense for someone who mainly walks. It is
            a simple machine, and simple machines are easy to check. Three things should shape the
            price you pay.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>No warranty.</strong> A used Weslo is sold as-is. If the motor board fails a
            month later, it is your problem.</li>
            <li><strong>A finite parts supply.</strong> Belts and keys are easy to find today. Boards
            and motors for discontinued models get scarcer every year.</li>
            <li><strong>Modest specifications.</strong> Two manual incline positions, a small motor
            and a 275 lb rating on the G 5.9 by Weslo’s published figure. It was never a runner’s
            machine.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our Cadence G 5.9 review suggests that a working, tested example is worth somewhere in the
            $150 to $300 range, and that above that a new budget treadmill with a warranty is the
            better buy. Run any used treadmill under power before paying, at a walk and a jog, and
            work through our <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill checklist</a>.
            Ask for the WLTL model number in advance, so you can check belt and key availability
            before you travel.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="choosing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Choosing in the Old Weslo Price Band</h2>
<p>
            Whatever you buy instead, the questions that decided whether a Weslo suited you still
            apply, and they narrow the alternatives quickly.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Walking or jogging?</strong> For walking, a 43-inch belt such as the THERUN’s is
            enough for most people. For regular jogging, look for 50 inches or more, as on the
            Goplus and the XTERRA.</li>
            <li><strong>Incline by hand or by button?</strong> If you walk at one steady gradient,
            manual incline is fine and cheaper. If you want hills that change during a walk, buy
            powered incline; every alternative here except the XTERRA has it.</li>
            <li><strong>Classes or no classes?</strong> The NordicTrack and ProForm machines are built
            around iFIT’s paid classes. If you will never subscribe, you are paying for features you
            will not use, and a simpler machine is better value.</li>
            <li><strong>How heavy are you?</strong> Ratings on this page run from 265 lb to 300 lb
            where stated. Choose one with a comfortable margin above your weight.</li>
            <li><strong>Where will it live?</strong> All the alternatives fold, but they weigh 104 to
            166 lb by Amazon’s figures. Check the folded footprint and whether the deck can be moved
            by one person.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/treadmill-buying-guide-2026/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill buying guide</a> goes through these choices in more depth, with the figures to look for at each budget.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-replaced-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What Replaced Weslo Within iFIT</h2>
<p>
            iFIT has not named a direct successor to Weslo, but its cheapest treadmills now carry the
            NordicTrack and ProForm names. On Amazon on 9 November 2026 the lowest-priced were the
            NordicTrack T Series 5 at $469.27 and the ProForm Carbon TL at $598. Both are folding
            machines with a 10 mph top speed and a powered 0 to 10% incline, and both are built to
            work with iFIT’s subscription classes, which change speed and incline for you.
          </p>
          <p>
            That subscription is the biggest difference from a Weslo. A Weslo was a self-contained
            machine with a basic console. The T Series 5 and Carbon TL work without a membership, but
            their listings tie their headline features, from auto-adjusting workouts to heart-rate
            control, to an iFIT Train membership. If you want a treadmill that is simple and
            subscription-free, the budget brands below them in our ranking are closer to the old
            Weslo idea. Our comparison of <a href="/nordictrack-vs-proform/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack and ProForm</a> explains how iFIT’s two remaining consumer brands differ.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes With Weslo Treadmills</h2>
          <p>Most come from treating a discontinued brand as if it were still current.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Ordering a belt by model name</h3>
              <p>The G 5.9 and G 5.9i take different belts, and older Cadence models have several variants. Order by the WLTL model number on the frame sticker, and measure the old belt if in doubt.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Paying new-machine money for a used one</h3>
              <p>A used Weslo has no warranty and a shrinking parts supply. Above roughly $300, a new budget treadmill with a warranty is the better buy.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Replacing the belt when the board has failed</h3>
              <p>Surging speed, a burning smell or a tripping breaker point to the motor, controller or wiring, not the belt. Diagnose before buying parts, and stop using a machine that shows those signs.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Trusting a listing that calls itself Weslo</h3>
              <p>Some listings use Weslo or Cadence in their titles for parts or for unrelated machines. Check the brand line, the seller and the model number before assuming a listing is a new Weslo treadmill.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Weslo’s treadmills are effectively a used-market brand now:
            Amazon sells parts for them, not the machines, and we could not confirm a new one at
            Walmart either.</strong> If you own a working Cadence, a belt and a key are cheap and worth
            buying; if the electronics fail, replace the treadmill.
          </p>
          <p>
            For a new machine in the same spirit, the NordicTrack T Series 5 is iFIT’s current entry
            treadmill, the XTERRA TRX1000 is closest in format, and Goplus and THERUN offer powered
            incline for less. Our <a href="/best-treadmills-under-500/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">best treadmills under $500</a> guide covers more options in this price band.
          </p>
        </div>

